WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.180
Times of Lore, the fantasy role-playing game filled with endless fantastic excitement.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.480
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.420
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:32.700
And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.020
Is it cheating to call a fantasy role-playing game fantastic excitement?

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.540
Like, fantastic fantasy?

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.720
Like, there's no other word there?

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.120
I don't know if it's cheating.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.660
Sean looks at you and says, this is just what they did.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.200
Like, this is just marketing, man.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.500
Like, this is just how you sell video games.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:00.320
And Mike replied that he hopes that the whole game doesn't talk to you like that.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.260
Every time.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.780
Joe said, starting with a closed parenthesis instead of an open parenthesis.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.220
Oh, is that what Joe said?

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.360
I'm thinking about what I actually said.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:15.700
And that's what Joe said. Oh, yeah.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:22.580
You are obviously speaking to the fact that they do start with a closed parenthesis instead of an open parenthesis.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.500
As before consoles...

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.360
They only designed the closed parenthesis.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:33.580
Yeah. As before consoles could have the compute power to generate an open parenthesis.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.080
Yeah. Or, not parenthesis, quotation.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:44.080
I wouldn't have even found it something to joke about if it just said, like, he said and he says, like, over and over.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:51.320
But it's, like, adding that sentence fragment that this person looks at you and says.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.860
Like, I don't think that's the detail we need.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.040
That they look at you first.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.420
And also, they're giving such extreme detail to then, like, really short responses.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.360
Yeah. I don't know anything about that.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.060
The old man looked at you and said no.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.720
Yeah. Like, it's, like, just say no.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.300
And I get that they're trying to, like, make it this way.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.060
It's, like, you're reading a story or something.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:17.520
But there's just not enough happening in these text dialogue boxes to, like, warrant this.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.160
Like, it's not a novel.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:27.460
Everything is... All these discussions are pretty short and require you to, like, continue pressing them on, like, all right, so tell me what rumors you know.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:28.980
Let's chit-chat.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:29.960
Here's a question.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.660
Actually, I want to stay here the night.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:45.580
You know, it's, like, to have all those different responses lends itself to short responses, not, you know, oh, here's, like, a little page in a book to set up what the person said.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:46.900
I honestly took it.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.460
The fact that they look at you first and then say something.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:58.560
It's almost as if you said something so idiotic that they have to make it very obvious that they're, like, slowly turning their head towards you.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.360
I mean, like, I don't know anything about that.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:02.940
I didn't consider that.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.120
We don't know what we start with as an intelligence stat in the game.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.500
We might have said something completely incoherent.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.960
We should probably give listeners a little more context.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.720
It is kind of interesting.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.040
I like that we just went right into it, just start bashing the game with its dialogue and everything.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:27.380
But Times of Lore, which I feel like it did give itself away, is one of those RPGs on the NES.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.260
And it is based on nothing.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.860
It is an original fantasy world.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.180
The world is called Albareth.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.800
And it was made by Chris Roberts at Origin Systems.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.300
Our favorite game developer.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.240
Yeah. We'll get into it.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.640
In 1988, this game was made.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.940
And it was not made for NES at that time, but popular enough that it would get a port.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:02.660
That name, Chris Roberts, though, I mean, that's the same Chris Roberts who made Wing Commander a year before this game came to the NES.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:09.720
That's 1990. And I played Wing Commander for the very first time at LI Retro earlier this year on a computer.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.460
And, you know, I got to say, it was quite fun.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.360
It felt short and everything.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.740
And it was definitely more of like a, this is just a cool space tech demo thing.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.500
But I think that, like, you know, Wing Commander does seem like something you can build a whole career around.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:30.680
Enough so that decades later, the same Chris Roberts would go on to create Star Citizen.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.360
That game that is still not out.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:34.820
Would he go on to create it?

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.000
Yeah. He will go on to create it.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.700
Right, right. He will go on to create this game, allegedly.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.900
It should release before he passes.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:53.800
But, like, this is insane that, you know, Times of Lore is connected to the most expensive video game of all time.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.260
I honestly see it.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:06.740
I can see it. With just the kind of attempt in scope that this game kind of has, it seems like this is just how he operates.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.060
I would be inclined to agree about the scope here.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:12.820
Like, it is pushing a lot.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:20.860
But now that we've been exposed to Ultima 3 and 4 as well, and knowing that those games, like, you know, came later as ports to the NES.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.560
So they were also, like, around this time.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:31.680
I feel like Ultima 4, especially with the whole, like, morality system thing, was doing something a lot grander than what Times of Lore is laying out here.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:40.680
Times of Lore is really more of a, like, less of an RPG and more of a, like, giant fetch quest.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.680
Yeah. And it's like, it's almost like a talking simulator.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.640
It's like the conversations are more.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:47.720
Talking simulator. Yeah.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.180
The conversations are more important than the actual, like, fighting.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.420
Or, you know, there's not, like, it's not really an RPG.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.520
I mean, there's not, like, leveling up, really.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:00.260
You know, like, there's not really any reason to kill anything other than to pick up some items.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:02.000
I found that out the hard way.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:03.460
I killed a lot of things.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:09.240
Just as I was, like, wandering around, and I was like, oh, I'm not getting experience points for this.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.420
Oh. Oh, you were.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:17.000
So I, um, I killed a lot of things immediately because I pressed the wrong button.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:18.540
Um. Oh, well, yeah.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:19.920
I killed a lot of, like, friendly people.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.580
Yeah. And then just decided, like, what would it be like to kill this whole town?

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:25.140
And then you could just kind of do that.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.800
Um. But that's not what you're supposed to do.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.140
That will softlock the game.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.920
Yada, yada. But. You were doing more of a Fallout run.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.860
It was, like, it was just an experiment.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.980
I was like, oh, they're going to take me down eventually.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.340
But they didn't.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.400
Um. But just to go back to the scope thing, though.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.720
The fact that you can kill the whole town.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:45.920
Not something I thought I'd see here.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:48.500
Um. Whether it's intended or not.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:53.400
Like, the fact that you can swing your weapon at an NPC and they will die.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:57.220
Uh. I don't think that I've seen that concept yet.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:06:59.620
Um. And then.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:02.380
Like, have we had a game with a day night cycle?

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.280
Uh. That just sort of happens.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:05.640
Like, while you play.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:07.240
Like. Castlevania 2.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:08.620
Ah, shit. True.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.200
I'm joking. That's the pause screen thing.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.600
Where it's like, you know. Oh, what a horrible night to have occurs.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.040
I mean, that does happen, though.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:18.940
Yeah. But it switches to, like, a completely different type of, you know.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:20.980
So, it's like, now there's enemies everywhere and stuff.

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:25.240
So, I do agree that, like, this is interesting that all of a sudden it just becomes night.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.260
But then it also is like, how long is night?

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.920
You know? Like, how long do I have to, like, walk around?

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.160
Yeah. To figure. Well, I was surprised to hear that answer, you know.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:39.180
Because I definitely would have appreciated if it just happened a little bit faster.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.940
If day and night cycles were in this game were just a little bit faster.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.240
Because there's a specific, like, almost the first quest you get.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:48.400
I arrive there and the guy is asleep.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:49.840
So, I'm like, oh.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.460
All right. So, I'm just, like, killing enemies.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:54.680
And I'm like, is it daytime yet?

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:55.860
Like, what's happening?

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:57.900
And I, yeah, several minutes had to go.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:07:59.080
Several real-time minutes.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.100
Yeah. Not several game minutes.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:04.480
Like, several real-time minutes of my life had to go before this guy was going to wake up.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.260
But doesn't that make you feel.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:08.680
I was there. Yeah, you were there.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.020
I was there in the times of lore.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:14.800
Yeah. It's very life sim.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.340
And I, like, as you guys are laying this out, I am.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:21.800
Okay, yeah. I guess it is deeper.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.400
Because when he first said, like, oh, you know, there's so much, like, scope to it.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.000
I was like, yeah, I guess maybe in, like, the map is big.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.260
But, yeah, as you're laying it out, I'm like, oh, yeah, totally.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.080
There's all this scope.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.540
But I didn't really feel it while I was playing it.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:39.120
I don't know. I think it's just because the moment-to-moment gameplay never, you know, and I didn't try

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.480
to take out a whole town and, like, see how that affected things.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.060
And that might have, like, made me realize, oh, wow, there's, like, a lot more going on

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:52.700
here. But in what the game is, like, wanting you to do, I didn't get, I felt that in the, like,

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:54.740
conversations that could, like,

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:58.340
yeah, you can unlock new paths in the conversations and things like that.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:01.420
But as far as, like, walking around, I was just like, this is too big.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:05.240
It's like the scope is just like, oh, there's, like, a bunch of empty space over here.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:09.080
Takes me a really long time to shuffle my feet through all this.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:14.100
And then there's, like, a big open space over here with, like, not a lot of landmarks.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.980
So, like, that kind of bugged me.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.200
But, like, yeah, I'm hearing all these things you're saying about, like, what you can do,

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.180
that the fact that there's, like, yeah, the day-night cycle and that it actually affects

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:27.460
things. And it is pretty ambitious.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:35.440
Someone mentioned the fact that you can, like, attack NPCs.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.120
Also, you talk to NPCs.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:42.980
And I just would be remiss if I didn't bring up the fact that the button to talk to the

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:48.400
NPCs, the button to open your menus, your menu, the first button you have to hit is, like,

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.700
let's say it's B, and then from then on it's A.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.260
So the whole time you're talking, you're pressing A, A, A, A.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:56.980
But if you hit A first, you stab them.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:58.180
And then they're your enemy.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:00.000
Why would they do that?

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:01.740
Just make it one button the whole time.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.280
It was very funny.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:09.780
And just, again, like, at that point, like, it's when you're playing Grand Theft Auto and

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.760
you may, like, you accidentally get one star by, like, running somebody over.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:16.880
Like, no, now you're going to kill the entire Los Santos Police Department.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.460
Like, it felt like that.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:22.360
And then I had to reset because, you know, nothing had happened.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:25.380
But, yeah, very silly.

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:31.220
I think it's important, and I hate that this is going to be the game that, like, is going

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:35.060
to get some kind of benefit of the doubt from me and that I didn't bring this up in, like,

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:40.860
any of the Ultima games or some of the, like, first-person dungeon crawling RPGs that we've

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:46.520
had. But, like, it is important to remember that Times of Lore is definitely not a game that you

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:52.380
are supposed to, like, rent a blockbuster, read the manual, be like, yep, I got this,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:54.640
and then complete in, like, an hour and a half.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:11:00.600
Like, this is the kind, this is a slow game that you are supposed to play, like, over a

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:06.080
long period of time where you just kind of, like, are enjoying being in this world.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:14.260
And, like, we have all of the knowledge of what comes, you know, 30 years onwards in gaming

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:17.520
to be, like, obviously there are better games in this.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:24.160
But at the time, if you are just, like, looking for, you know, like, oh, wow, this guy, Chris

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:29.860
Roberts created an entire world that, like, I can literally on foot travel to and find

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:30.860
all these different towns.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.120
And one day I might just walk into a new town I had never seen before and talk to new people

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:37.200
and maybe pick up a new rumor, right?

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:42.080
Like, that is all of the interesting stuff of this game that works.

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:48.260
But I feel like the core loop of being this character does suck.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:51.020
Like, it does suck.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:54.400
Like, even just think about the walking animation that they gave him.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.180
The way it sounds. Yeah, the way it sounds when you walk.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:12:01.080
Yeah, exactly. This is such a big world and you are walking, like, I don't know if they're trying to, like,

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:05.780
they're trying to, like, actually measure, well, like, this is how far he'd be able to step.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:07.920
But, like, guys, just give him a little bit of a bigger step.

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:09.220
Like, maybe he's in a hurry.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:10.760
Well, that's why you gotta find new shoes.

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:12.040
You can find new shoes.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:13.640
Yes, that's true. That is true.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:15.580
Yeah, I used a guide.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:23.720
But to your point, though, like, the first game that I ever played that made me feel what I imagine this game would make me feel,

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:32.700
like, if I had, like, the map for, I don't know, Alderath or whatever you call it, in front of me while I was playing,

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:35.580
like, that's how I felt when I had Morrowind.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:41.340
Like, I had the big fold-out Morrowind map and I was like, wow, I guess I'm just gonna walk over there.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:43.840
And I can see myself doing that.

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:49.460
And, yeah, I probably would find the way that you walk in this game very annoying.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:56.360
But, like, I don't know if you guys played Morrowind any time recently, but, like, that game feels like shit to play.

00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:03.620
Like, it is, like, to swing a weapon is, it feels like you're missing 80% of the time.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:05.380
Like, you do move very slowly.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:10.440
But sometimes if you're actually kind of, like, getting into the world, that doesn't matter.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.100
You live in this world now.

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:16.380
Obviously, that didn't quite have the same effect on me.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:28.440
Today, I'm just kind of assuming that would be how I felt because my brain has been ruined by fast dopamine hits the last 15 years.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:31.860
But, you know, that's neither here nor there.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:38.100
You guys talk. But I have to imagine there was more happening in Morrowind even when you weren't in the towns.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:44.140
And I think one thing that this game has a problem with is that it is a rather large map.

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:51.280
Yes. But a lot of it is just empty areas filled with enemies that are just randomly spawning in.

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:57.000
So the combat system needs to be a hell of a lot better than what it actually is.

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.000
It is a trivial combat system.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:02.880
You will pretty much kill everybody.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:05.920
This is a very easy game.

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:07.660
You could kind of Dark Souls it.

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:10.560
And just run by everyone once you get those shoes.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.480
Which is kind of weird.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:19.040
And there's only a couple times when you're actually forced to fight.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:21.500
Let's unpack that for a second.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:23.500
You said Dark Souls it.

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:27.700
I feel like most people talk about Dark Souls because of the enemy encounters.

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:32.560
And you're suggesting that you could Dark Souls it in the sense of, like, on the runbacks, right?

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:35.880
Yeah. It's like, I just got to get back to the boss and I just want to skip all the enemies again.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:41.920
Like, I don't think anybody's playing Dark Souls with the intention of just, like, skipping the most important part of the game, right?

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:45.700
Sure. But I guess that doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:14:45.840 --> 00:15:00.160
But when I think about running through encounters and kind of just ignoring everybody, I only really think about doing that in Dark Souls trying to get back to either my drop souls or to a boss.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:05.440
So, that's just how it feels moving around in this game.

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:06.740
You don't have to fight anything.

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:08.740
You don't get anything for fighting.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:11.240
I mean, sometimes they drop items or gold.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:12.340
But it's not a guarantee.

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:15.200
This isn't like a JRPG where you're always going to get something.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:18.720
Whether it be experience or gold or items, whatever.

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:22.160
It's more just stuff in the way.

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:30.620
And if you know where you're going, which, like, if you didn't have a walkthrough, you might not know where you're going.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.900
So, maybe you would kind of be forced to fight more often.

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:39.820
But, like, right now, it's just kind of a broken system.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:44.140
Yeah, I'd want, like, there to be something else you can pick up.

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:52.060
Because pretty much all you're picking up from killing people are things to help you heal from killing people, you know, for the most part.

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:53.520
Or gold. Food.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:58.180
But, like, yeah, I'd want a little more incentive to fight, I guess.

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:07.960
But, you know, I am coming around to the idea of this game, like, in its time being this thing that you can, like, live with.

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:11.120
I hadn't really, like, for some reason, I hadn't really thought of it like that.

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:13.540
And I'm, like, always thinking of games like that.

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:15.900
Like, oh, like, at the time, though, I would have sat with it.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:17.200
And, like, this one, it just didn't hit me like that.

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:21.500
But, like, it really is, like, kind of interesting to think about.

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:25.980
Like, yeah, I could just be playing this for, like, every once in a while for months.

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:27.540
And, like, oh, I found a new rumor in this place.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:29.140
Oh, now I'm in this town I never saw.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:36.760
I think it was just the, like, yeah, the moment-to-moment gameplay loop just wasn't keeping me absorbed enough to, like, get me there.

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:42.340
Right now in 2026 to get me to that point where I'm, like, interested enough in that.

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:45.500
Joe, you had described it a bit as a talking simulator.

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:51.620
Do you think that the clues that people are giving you as to what to do next is helpful?

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:59.140
Or do you find it to be, like, all right, now I've got to wander around a little bit to see if I can find the next place that I need to go?

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:10.000
Well, I think, I mean, I think it can be helpful, again, in the context of, like, not, like, today where we need, you know, a blue glowing line on the ground showing us exactly where to go.

00:17:10.180 --> 00:17:12.000
Where it's like, yeah, okay, I got the idea.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:14.940
Now I have to, like, kind of have a mix of both.

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:17.540
Like, use the clue but also kind of figure it out myself.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:20.420
And, like, gives you more an excuse to live in the world longer.

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:24.220
And to, like, kind of expand the time that it takes to play the game.

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:26.920
It's like, you've got to figure, you've got to find these things.

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:28.240
And I think the clues were nice.

00:17:28.340 --> 00:17:34.840
I think what bothered me a little bit was how many people, at least at first, just, like, don't tell you anything at all.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:41.660
And I did eventually realize you've got to kind of, like, finesse it a little bit with, like, having certain type of conversation first and, like, becoming their friend, I guess.

00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:43.080
Ask them how the weather's been, yeah.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:48.080
Yeah. But, like, yeah, there's so many times, like, okay, there's all these little houses.

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:50.340
I can go to another town and there's all these houses.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:51.620
There's got to be someone in this house.

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:53.060
There's got to be someone in this castle, whatever.

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:56.980
It takes me 20 minutes to walk over to it because you walk so slow.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:01.020
And then I get in there and there's two people in it and they both just say, like, oh, I don't know anything about anything at all.

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:03.960
I'm just here. Like, what's your, what's, why are you in this game?

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:08.980
But I'm sure that there was, like, ways, like, thinking back on it now.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:16.920
I'm like, I probably could have, like, gone back to those, like, starting town and, like, talked to those people in ways where, like, eventually I would get something out of them.

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:18.680
Or at least more of them than I did.

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:30.640
But it felt like almost everyone I would run into and I would waste the time of opening my menu to go select talk to then talk to them to then have to then be told that they look at me before they say something to then just have what they say be.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:34.060
Oh, yeah, I don't, I don't know anything.

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:40.320
I have been that guy on the street that, like, some, like, you know, the hero walks up to me, asks for directions.

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:42.840
Like, how do I get to Fifth Avenue?

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:47.040
And I'm like, I actually can't help you.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:50.780
I mean, I would point, I would point if it was Fifth Avenue.

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:54.800
But sometimes I genuinely don't know how to get to where they're asking.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:00.440
And they kind of give me that look that, Joe, you just had of, like, well, what good are you?

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:01.580
Like, why are you even here?

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:02.900
Like, if you're not going to help me.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:05.080
So I think that's actually kind of realistic.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:10.880
Your reaction is how the hero would act towards these NBCs.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:11.880
NBCs. Yeah, okay.

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:15.000
So I was really, like, I were in that world.

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:18.440
Yeah. Yeah, yeah. So I guess I was more absorbed in this game than I thought.

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:27.780
The game also does an interesting job of, like, telling, it plays, like, a sound when something, uh, when they mention something important.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:29.780
Right. Yeah. Make sure you pay attention.

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:31.600
Pay attention. This part matters.

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:36.260
And I think that, hey, that's at least something, you know, especially for a game that is very text heavy.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:40.620
It is nice to be like, okay, yeah, yeah, but this actually does mean something.

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:43.680
Like, like, like, please, please make note of this.

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:55.240
And I like that. Did you, did any of you, I mean, like, I think I've gotten in the habit of in these games where they kind of, like, ask you to take up the cause of the game.

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:57.040
And you have the ability to say no.

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:09.940
I said no. Well, that's interesting because we've talked about this before on this very show about games letting you say no and then just being like, that's not an acceptable answer and just forcing you, like, why tell me to say no?

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:17.080
Obviously, I know, I, I will let you finish the thought here, but I just, I had to test it because we've talked about it in the past.

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:19.200
So I also said no, but what happens?

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:21.160
So it depends on who you're talking to.

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:28.860
So if you talk to the first guy, the prior, and he's like, won't you please take up this quest?

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:32.480
And if you say no, he'll be like, oh, all right.

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:35.700
I thought, I thought you were better than that.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:38.600
And then you can, like, actually ask him, like, how his day is going.

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:39.740
And he'll be like, it's nice.

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:43.000
And the guy just rejected me.

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:47.680
Yeah. But then he will just ask the question again and you can say yes.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:56.460
But if you then get to the point where you're talking to, like, I don't know, the vice king, because the real king's gone.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:02.820
If you talk to him and then you turn him down, he just throws you in jail.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:08.680
And then you, like, kind of actually, it cuts to you being in jail and then you just drop dead in jail.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:11.340
Which I thought was funny.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:14.280
And hopefully. But it is a game over.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:15.920
It's funny. I hope you save.

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:16.840
You actually have to, like, load up.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:25.360
Yeah, right. But it's also funny because it's like, that's like a little, like, it's like a, it's like a macro version of what happens every time you talk to someone.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:29.040
It's like, it could have just said, okay, game over, or you died, or he killed you.

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:33.340
Instead, it, like, gives you a cut scene first of you being in jail and then being, like, you died.

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:35.500
It's just like, all right, just let me move on.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:52.760
It was certainly a choice, though, in a game that is, you know, trying to help you, like, live in the world and roam the world to have this giant candle health bar just persistent in the bottom right at all times.

00:21:52.760 --> 00:22:01.080
Like, I'm not sure, like, I don't know if I'm asking for just, like, the standard bar or whatever, or if it should have been buried in the, in a menu that you press start to, like, check your HP.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:08.420
But this giant, like, ever-burning candle thing that, like, as you take damage, you know, it slowly starts melting away.

00:22:08.420 --> 00:22:13.120
And if you drink the right potion, it will refill the candle and grow it back up.

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:21.680
It's just, like, it's so in the way of the, of the game that I, I just, I, I felt like there had to be a better way.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:27.100
I mean, I guess it's cute, but it's also not necessarily, like, what is the flame supposed to represent?

00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:30.500
Yeah, it's because it's, like, yeah, it's, like, yeah, it's your food.

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:32.540
It's your health.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:34.620
It's your health, yeah, yeah.

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:38.320
Your health does go down when you, when you don't eat, yeah.

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:41.340
Food replenishes it, I guess, yeah.

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:44.260
So, I guess it's not your food, but I don't know why I saw it that way.

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.020
But, like, I think it looks really cool.

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:49.840
Yeah, this is, like, that classic skeuomorphic design, guys.

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:56.100
Like, and, yes, it does actually get in the way of text boxes if a text box goes over there.

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.000
But that's just part of the charm.

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:00.320
It also gets in the way of enemies, though.

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:02.440
Like, if they're entering from that side of the screen.

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:07.340
Like, it just, it's, the fact that it's actually covering things is just, like, that cannot be the best spot for it.

00:23:07.460 --> 00:23:16.180
Yeah. Well, the other thing, though, like, the one thing that I did think was impressive about it is it is constantly, like, you know, it will go down when you're not getting hit.

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:17.380
It'll go down because you're not eating.

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:19.020
And, like, now I'm hungry, right?

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:25.400
Yeah, when I'm watching a playthrough of it and I'm, like, literally just focusing directly on the candle, obviously you can see it go down.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:28.940
But, like, it does go down pretty slowly and very, like, subtly.

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:32.420
Where, to me, I'm just, like, it's almost like watching a real candle burn.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:35.060
Every once in a while I glance over, I'm like, oh, it's a little smaller than I remember.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:37.840
It's a little smaller. But it's, like, I don't ever see it going down.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:42.260
This is a very accurate candle simulator at its core.

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:47.340
Yeah, this was, like, honestly the most impressive, like, part of this game.

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:53.840
I think this is why they couldn't do the open quotation marks because they put too much power into this candle.

00:23:55.100 --> 00:24:00.140
And maybe it also has something to do with why it takes a long time for the game to register.

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:01.460
You're trying to turn around.

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:03.420
Yeah, that was really weird.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:04.880
Why would it be like that?

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:08.840
It takes a lot of strength to turn around.

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:10.900
Have you ever tried it? That's true.

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:12.000
I've never tried it, actually.

00:24:12.260 --> 00:24:13.720
Yeah, you always just go forward.

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:15.700
That's one thing we know about you.

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:26.080
It's almost like, you know, how modern games ended up making everything feel, like, really shit because they're trying to be, like, realistic in how people move.

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:28.160
So now, like, you can't just turn on a dime.

00:24:28.340 --> 00:24:30.440
It's like they decided to do that 30 years ago.

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:32.880
Yeah, except for, like, way longer.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:37.620
It's like you have to walk backwards for, like, 40 steps before you can turn around.

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:43.980
Oh, at the beginning of the game, you get to choose whether you want to be a knight, a Valkyrie, or a barbarian.

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:45.200
Does that matter?

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:46.160
I don't think so.

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:48.480
Yeah, I don't think it's. I think it's just the sprite.

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:50.940
Yeah, I mean, I chose Valkyrie.

